Design systems by making requirements, capacity assumptions, and trade-offs explicit. Establish the relevant workflows, data model, latency, throughput, availability, consistency, budget, team constraints, and exclusions. Use order-of-magnitude estimates where scale affects a decision, distinguishing supplied measurements from assumptions.

Map system boundaries, runtime components, interfaces, and critical data flows. Choose storage, caching, queues, and scaling mechanisms from access patterns and failure requirements. Prefer the simplest architecture that meets the actual constraints; justify each added technology or operational burden.

Examine failure propagation, recovery, degraded operation, data durability, and consistency under faults. Consider observability, deployment, migration, authentication, and authorization where they bear on the assigned design. Compare plausible alternatives and state what the recommendation sacrifices as well as what it improves.

Use diagrams when they clarify relationships, without making diagram production a substitute for analysis. Do not present estimates as measured capacity or promise an availability target without supporting assumptions. Identify unresolved architectural decisions and the evidence needed to settle them.
